      {
        "id": 116632,
        "name": "phantaskippy",
        "username": "phantaskippy",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/phantaskippy/{size}/1897_2.png",
        "created_at": "2014-09-23T02:27:53.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"tal342\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/t/f05b48/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> tal342:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I don't understand this at all.&nbsp; If the heroes are doing nothing to stop you, why does it take more or less turns to score 3 shards depending on the number of heroes?&nbsp; You have the same number of citizens after all, just less turns when there are less heroes.&nbsp;</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Each Citizen can only act once per round. &nbsp;So with more heroes you get more citizen turns per round, but you are forced to use more of the citizens. &nbsp;With just 2 citizens you only ever use 3 citizens, the delay with three citizens is because you don't get to break up turns as efficiently as you can with 2 per round, since once a citizen takes a turn you have to wait for the next round.</p>\n<p>Higher numbers of citizens work better when the heroes are actually trying, because it gives you a little redundancy to handle incaps and dropped shards.</p>",
        "post_number": 22,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T03:49:06.202Z",
        "reply_count": 0,
        "reply_to_post_number": null,
